{"name":"Soylent Green","hex":"#578363","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"soylent green","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any foodstuff of dubious nature or origin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/soylent_green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Soylent Green","blocks":[{"text":"Soylent Green is a 1973 American dystopian thriller film directed by Richard Fleischer, and starring Charlton Heston, Leigh Taylor-Young, and Edward G. Robinson in his final film role. It is loosely based on the 1966 science-fiction novel Make Room! Make Room! by Harry Harrison, with a plot that combines elements of science fiction and a police procedural. The story follows a murder investigation in a dystopian future of dying oceans and year-round humidity caused by the greenhouse effect, with the resulting pollution, depleted resources, poverty, crime, and overpopulation."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/1/13/Soylent_green.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soylent_Green","wikidata":"Q426196"},{"title":"Soylent (meal replacement)","blocks":[{"text":"Soylent is a brand of meal replacement powders and shakes produced by Starco Brands, Inc. The brand was founded as Soylent Nutrition, Inc. in 2013 and is headquartered in Los Angeles, California. The company developed a following initially in Silicon Valley and received early financial backing from Andreessen Horowitz and GV. In 2021, Soylent announced that it had become profitable starting in 2020. In 2023, Starco Brands acquired the company."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Soylent.svg/330px-Soylent.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soylent_(meal_replacement)","wikidata":"Q13384640"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Harry_Harrison_in_Moscow.jpg/500px-Harry_Harrison_in_Moscow.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Harry_Harrison_in_Moscow.jpg/1280px-Harry_Harrison_in_Moscow.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Harry_Harrison_in_Moscow.jpg/500px-Harry_Harrison_in_Moscow.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Harry Harrison, whose 1966 novel Make Room! Make Room! was adapted into Soylent Green, had no creative control over the film and was of mixed opinion on the final product.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHarry_Harrison_in_Moscow.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Edward_G._Robinson_at_the_New_York_premiere_of_The_Ten_Commandments.jpg/500px-Edward_G._Robinson_at_the_New_York_premiere_of_The_Ten_Commandments.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Edward_G._Robinson_at_the_New_York_premiere_of_The_Ten_Commandments.jpg/500px-Edward_G._Robinson_at_the_New_York_premiere_of_The_Ten_Commandments.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Edward G. Robinson was praised by critics for his performance in Soylent Green, which he completed filming 84 days before his death.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEdward_G._Robinson_at_the_New_York_premiere_of_The_Ten_Commandments.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Soylent_drink.jpg/500px-Soylent_drink.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Soylent_drink.jpg/1280px-Soylent_drink.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Soylent_drink.jpg/500px-Soylent_drink.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A Soylent package, along with the powder and resulting drink","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASoylent_drink.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Spanish_jewellery-Gold_and_emerald_pendant_at_VAM-01.jpg/500px-Spanish_jewellery-Gold_and_emerald_pendant_at_VAM-01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Spanish_jewellery-Gold_and_emerald_pendant_at_VAM-01.jpg/500px-Spanish_jewellery-Gold_and_emerald_pendant_at_VAM-01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&ut